Gerhard van Vught writes:

>I just very recently aquired the latest version of NetHack PC, having only
>played the very earlier Pc Hack version of the game. PC Hack could run on a
>88 based machine, but now I see NetHack only takes 286s and up. Is there 
>anywhere out there where I can obtain an 8088 version for NetHack PC? Or 
>from where I can obtain the source code for the DOS based version so that 
>compile it on my machine? I use Turbo C.

Check out the readme.pc file for some info on why this bloated monstrousity
doesn't run on an 8086/8088.  Basically, the code doesn't fit in low memory,
so it either has to use protected-mode or a flat memory model (which 
excluded pre-286's) or it has to use overlays.  Overlays work, but they're
slow, especially when things have to be swapped in from disk.  On a slow
computer this would make the game unplayable.  Realizing this, the PC 
dev people made two official binaries, one for 286+ (Microsoft C) and 
one for 386+ (DJGPP, better, because it's not overlaid).

You could, theoretically, as an exercise, compile an 8086/8088 version.
I wouldn't use an 8088-based machine to actually compile it, though, as
the compile time would probably be longer than the mean time between failures
on the computer, or at least the mean time between blackouts where you 
have the machine plugged in.  But let's say you wanted to do this.  
First, you'd download all the source code (over 3 megs) from the ftp 
site linc.cis.upenn.edu, under pub/NH3.1/source (or sources?)  That 
would involve downloading the files that tell you which files you need 
first, then downloading those.  (And you'd do all this outside linc's
prime hours.)  Then you'd borrow a faster computer to compile with, and
a different compiler.  Nobody can make Borland compilers do NetHack.  
(If you can, tell the PC dev team and they'll give you a medal.)  Then,
after a very long makefile executed a very long compilation, you'd 
carry your new 8086/8088 binaries over to your (probably low density)
floppy drive and load and run.  And it would take 10 or 20 seconds
per move, and you'd lament those hours you wasted getting this program
to compile, since the result is too slow to play.

Now, NetHack gets a little bigger with each version, and the sad story
above relates to 3.1.3, the current one.  If you're willing to travel
back in time a bit, maybe you can find a version of NetHack that works
fine on an 8088.  Not the newest and spiffiest, but newer than PC Hack.
And maybe some nice person has an already-compiled version ready for 
you.  And have you checked out the prices on used 286's lately?  Almost
free.  Not a great machine, but better than an 8088.

Good luck.
